diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7a8ecb4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,678 @@
+# Helpdesk API
+
+Rails 8.1 API-only backend with PostgreSQL, Puma, bcrypt passwords, bearer sessions,
+Action Mailer, and Action Mailbox. There are no browser cookies or server-rendered frontend pages.
+
+## Setup
+
+Ruby 4.0 and PostgreSQL are required. All environments connect using `DATABASE_URL`.
+
+```sh
+export DATABASE_URL='postgresql:///helpdesk_development?host=/var/lib/postgresql'
+bundle install
+bin/rails db:prepare
+bin/rails server -b 0.0.0.0
+```
+
+Use a URL appropriate to your PostgreSQL installation. `bin/setup --skip-server`
+also installs dependencies and prepares the database. `GET /up` is the health check.
+
+Configuration (see `.env.example`; export variables yourself):
+
+| Variable | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `DATABASE_URL` | Required | PostgreSQL connection |
+| `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL` | `http://localhost:3001` | Frontend link base and allowed CORS origin |
+| `HELPDESK_MAIL_FROM` | `support@example.test` | Sender for verification, reset, and invitation emails |
+| `HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` | `support.example.test` | Domain for business support and private ticket reply addresses |
+| `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD` | Required for relay | HTTP Basic password for Action Mailbox (username `actionmailbox`) |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS` | `localhost` in production | SMTP server; setting it enables SMTP in development too |
+| `SMTP_PORT` | `25` | SMTP port (e.g. `587` for submission with STARTTLS) |
+| `SMTP_DOMAIN` | `localhost` | SMTP HELO domain |
+| `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD` | Unset | Optional SMTP authentication |
+| `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` | `plain` when a username is set | SMTP authentication mechanism |
+
+Development writes outgoing messages to `tmp/mails/` by default. Tests use
+`ActionMailer::Base.deliveries`. Production uses SMTP; configure your provider
+before accepting signups. Existing account/portal mail delivery is synchronous.
+Inbound mail routing and email-ticket notifications use Active Job; see below.
+Production also needs `SECRET_KEY_BASE` (generate using `bin/rails secret`) and
+assumes an HTTPS-terminating reverse proxy, as configured by Rails.
+
+CORS allows the configured frontend origin, the supported API methods, and
+`Content-Type`, `Authorization`, and `X-Helpdesk-Account`. It does not allow
+arbitrary origins or cookie credentials. Optional path prefixes in the frontend
+URL are retained in links but omitted from the CORS origin.
+
+## Email conversations (Action Mailbox)
+
+Each business receives mail at `<subdomain>@HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN`, for example
+`acme@support.example.test`. Configure your mail provider/MX to relay **all addresses**
+at this domain, including `reply+...` ticket addresses, to:
+
+```text
+POST /rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails
+Content-Type: message/rfc822
+Authorization: Basic <base64 of actionmailbox:RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD>
+
+<raw RFC 822 message, not JSON>
+```
+
+For example, with the password exported in your shell:
+
+```sh
+curl --fail --user "actionmailbox:$RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD" \
+  --header 'Content-Type: message/rfc822' --data-binary @message.eml \
+  https://api.example.test/rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails
+```
+
+This is the standard Rails relay ingress; it does not use portal bearer tokens or
+`X-Helpdesk-Account`. Successful ingestion returns **204**, including ignored and
+duplicate messages. Missing/wrong credentials return **401**, and a wrong content
+type returns **415**. Use HTTPS and keep the relay password private. The upstream
+mail server must perform spam and sender-authentication checks (SPF/DKIM/DMARC);
+a matching From address alone is not proof that an SMTP sender owns that address.
+
+Behavior:
+
+- Recipients (including Cc and relay-provided original/forwarded recipients) identify
+  the business. Mail naming multiple businesses or multiple ticket reply addresses
+  is ignored rather than exposing a conversation to the wrong business.
+- A message to the plain business address creates a new open ticket with its subject
+  and decoded, non-attachment `text/plain` body. Multipart alternatives use plain
+  text, never HTML. Empty, HTML-only, attachment-only, malformed, automated, list,
+  and null-return-path mail is silently ignored, without sending bounces.
+- Customers are reused by normalized email within that business. A new customer
+  receives a verification email and a random, undisclosed password. Receiving mail
+  **does not verify** an account. To use the portal, confirm the verification link
+  and use the existing password-reset flow to choose a password. Existing customers
+  are not re-verified or sent a fresh verification link on every message.
+- Cancelled businesses, suspended customers, staff senders, and addresses reserved
+  by pending staff invitations cannot open tickets by email.
+- A committed staff public reply on an email-origin ticket is emailed only to its
+  requester, with an unguessable private `Reply-To` address at the support domain.
+  Internal notes, customer comments, status-only changes, and portal-origin tickets
+  do not trigger these notifications. Only the requester's From address can use the
+  private reply address. An accepted reply adds a public comment and reopens pending
+  or solved tickets. Closed tickets reject replies without reopening, forwarding to
+  a merge target, or creating another ticket. Reply addresses are not exposed in ticket JSON.
+- A new message to the **plain business address always starts a new ticket**, even
+  if it contains `In-Reply-To` or `References`. When the plain address and one private
+  address for the same business both appear, the private address identifies the ticket.
+- Accepted Message-IDs have a durable, database-unique receipt, committed atomically
+  with the comment. Retries with different transport headers/content cannot duplicate
+  tickets, comments, users, or verification emails. Ignored/failed deliveries do not
+  consume a Message-ID; even an identical rejected message can be retried after the
+  business becomes available. Without a Message-ID, Rails derives one from the raw
+  message checksum. Receipts outlive raw-message expiry and ticket deletion.
+
+Run `bin/rails db:prepare` to install the Active Storage, Action Mailbox, and helpdesk
+email migrations. Raw messages use Active Storage disk storage (`storage/` normally,
+`tmp/storage/` in test). Persist this directory or configure a shared Active Storage
+service before deploying multiple instances. Rails incinerates processed raw mail
+through a scheduled job after 30 days. Attachments are not imported into ticket comments.
+
+In **test**, jobs run inline, so the relay request processes the message and sends
+notifications before returning; no worker is needed. Scheduled incineration is disabled
+there. Development uses Rails' in-process async adapter. For reliable production
+processing, configure a durable Active Job adapter and run its workers for routing,
+mail delivery, Active Storage, and scheduled incineration; the default in-process
+adapter does not survive application restarts.
+
+## API
+
+Every endpoint below is under **`/api/v1`**. Send JSON with
+`Content-Type: application/json`. Except for business signup, every request must select
+its business with `X-Helpdesk-Account: acme`. Authenticated requests also require
+`Authorization: Bearer TOKEN`.
+
+### Signup and email verification
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/account/create
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{"account":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme","admin":{"name":"Jo","email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}}
+```
+
+Returns `201` with `account` and `user`. The user is the owner, has role `admin`,
+and starts with `verified: false`; signup does not create a session. Account
+creation, owner creation, and verification-token creation are transactional.
+Subdomains and emails are trimmed and lowercased. Email uniqueness is scoped to
+an account. Names cannot be blank. Passwords need at least 8 characters and can
+be at most 72 bytes (bcrypt's limit). Subdomains are 1–63 lowercase ASCII letters,
+digits, or hyphens, without a leading or trailing hyphen.
+
+The email contains:
+`http://localhost:3001/verify-email?account=acme&token=TOKEN`.
+The frontend submits the token, with the account header, to:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/email_verifications/confirm
+
+{"email_verification":{"token":"TOKEN"}}
+```
+
+Returns `200` with the verified `user`. Verification links expire after 24 hours.
+Resend using `POST /email_verifications` with
+`{"email_verification":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}`.
+
+### Sessions and profile
+
+| Method and path | JSON body | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `GET /portal` | None; public | `200 {"portal":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme"}}` |
+| `POST /sessions` | `{"session":{"email":"jo@acme.test","password":"my-first-password"}}` | `201` with `user`, `account`, and `session.token` |
+| `DELETE /session` | None | `204`; revokes only the presented session |
+| `GET /account` | None | `200` with `account` |
+| `GET /me` | None | `200` with `user` |
+| `PATCH /me` | `{"user":{"name":"Jo Smith"}}` | `200` with `user`; only the name can be updated |
+| `PUT /me/password` | `{"password_change":{"current_password":"my-first-password","password":"my-new-password"}}` | `204`; revokes **all** of that user's sessions, including the current one |
+
+Users must verify their email before login (invitation acceptance verifies it).
+Suspended users cannot sign in or use existing sessions. Sessions expire after 30 days.
+Accounts include `id`, `name`, `subdomain`, `cancelled_at`, `created_at`, and
+`updated_at`. Users include `id`, `account_id`, `name`, `email`, `role`, `verified`,
+`suspended`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`. Password digests and internal token records are
+never serialized. Session tokens are returned only when a session is created.
+
+### Customer registration
+
+Customers register in an existing, active business using its account header:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/registrations
+X-Helpdesk-Account: acme
+
+{"user":{"name":"Avery","email":"avery@example.test","password":"my-customer-password"}}
+```
+
+Returns `201 {"user":{...}}` and sends the existing verification email. The user
+always has role `customer`, is not an owner, and starts unverified with no session.
+Registration cannot grant staff access or select a different account in the body.
+Email uniqueness and password validation are the same as business signup.
+Customers use the same verification, sessions, `/me`, password-change, and
+password-recovery endpoints described above and below.
+
+### Team invitations
+
+Only verified, unsuspended admins (not just the owner) can manage invitations:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/invitations
+
+{"invitation":{"email":"sam@example.test","role":"agent"}}
+```
+
+Returns `201` with `invitation` and emails
+`/accept-invitation?account=acme&token=TOKEN` on `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL`.
+Only `admin` and `agent` are valid invitation roles. An invitation includes `id`,
+`email`, `role`, `status`, `expires_at`, `accepted_at`, `revoked_at`, `created_at`,
+and `updated_at`; tokens and their digests are never returned in these records.
+
+| Method and path | Success |
+| --- | --- |
+| `GET /invitations` | `200` with `invitations` and pagination metadata; optional `status` filter |
+| `GET /invitations/:id` | `200` with `invitation` |
+| `POST /invitations/:id/resend` | `200` with `invitation`; emails a new token |
+| `DELETE /invitations/:id` | `204`; revokes rather than deletes the record |
+
+Invitations expire after seven days. Statuses are `pending`, `accepted`, `revoked`,
+and `expired`. Resending a pending or expired invitation rotates its token and
+restarts expiry; the old link immediately stops working. Accepted or revoked
+invitations cannot be resent, and accepted invitations cannot be revoked.
+Revoking an already revoked invitation is harmless. A fresh invitation may be
+created after revocation or expiry if the email is still available.
+
+Emails are normalized and reserved within the business: an existing user or a
+pending invitation prevents another invitation to that email. Customer
+registration cannot claim an unexpired pending invitation's email. Expired and
+revoked invitations release the reservation. Lifecycle operations, registration,
+and acceptance run under the account lock to prevent competing requests.
+
+Preview and acceptance are public (no bearer token), but require the business's
+`X-Helpdesk-Account` header. The frontend first submits:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/invitation_previews
+
+{"invitation_preview":{"token":"TOKEN"}}
+```
+
+Returns `200`:
+
+```json
+{"invitation_preview":{"email":"sam@example.test","role":"agent","account":{"name":"Acme","subdomain":"acme"},"expires_at":"2026-10-15T12:00:00.000Z"}}
+```
+
+Then submit:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/invitation_acceptances
+
+{"invitation_acceptance":{"token":"TOKEN","name":"Sam","password":"my-team-password"}}
+```
+
+Returns `201` with `user` and `account`. Email and role come only from the
+invitation. The new user is verified and can sign in immediately using the normal
+sessions endpoint; acceptance creates no session and sends no verification email.
+Invalid names/passwords do not consume the invitation. Links are single-use,
+tenant-scoped, stored only as digests, and invalid at the exact expiry time.
+Invalid, expired, revoked, or consumed links return `422` for preview/acceptance.
+Cancelled businesses cannot manage, preview, or accept invitations.
+
+### People and staff choices
+
+Only admins can use the people-management endpoints:
+
+| Method and path | Success |
+| --- | --- |
+| `GET /users` | `200` with `users` and pagination metadata; optional `role` and `suspended=true/false` filters |
+| `GET /users/:id` | `200` with `user` |
+| `PATCH /users/:id` | `200` with `user`; accepts `name` and/or boolean `suspended` |
+
+For example, `{"user":{"suspended":true}}` suspends a user and `false` restores
+access. Suspension immediately revokes every session and clears all ticket
+assignees, but retains ticket groups, memberships, and defaults. Restoring access does not restore sessions or assignments. Suspended
+users cannot use password reset or email-verification links. Suspending the last
+verified, unsuspended admin returns `422`; another verified admin must remain.
+These endpoints cannot change role, email, ownership, verification, or password.
+
+`GET /staff` is available to admins and agents and returns `staff` plus pagination
+metadata: only verified, unsuspended staff in this business, suitable for assignment
+controls. Existing `member` users retain their staff permissions for compatibility,
+but cannot be invited or manage people. **Users, staff, and invitations sort by ID
+ascending.** Invalid role, suspension, or status filters return `400`.
+
+### Groups and memberships
+
+Groups organize work, not access: every staff member can still see every ticket
+in their business, whether or not they belong to its group. Admins manage groups
+and memberships; all staff can browse them. Customers cannot use these routes.
+All records and lookups are scoped to the selected business.
+
+| Method and path | Success |
+| --- | --- |
+| `POST /groups` | `201` with `group`; accepts `{"group":{"name":"Billing"}}` |
+| `GET /groups` | `200` with `groups` and pagination metadata |
+| `GET /groups/:id` | `200` with `group` |
+| `PATCH /groups/:id` | `200` with `group`; accepts a new `name` |
+| `DELETE /groups/:id` | `204`; removes memberships and clears the group and assignee on its tickets |
+| `POST /group_memberships` | `201` with `group_membership` |
+| `GET /group_memberships` | `200` with `group_memberships` and pagination metadata; optional `group_id` and `user_id` filters combine |
+| `GET /group_memberships/:id` | `200` with `group_membership` |
+| `DELETE /group_memberships/:id` | `204`; clears this user's assignments in this group, retaining the ticket group |
+| `PUT /group_memberships/:id/make_default` | `200` with the chosen `group_membership`; no body required |
+
+Group names are trimmed, nonblank, and at most 120 characters. Group JSON contains
+`id`, `account_id`, `name`, `created_at`, and `updated_at`. Membership JSON contains
+`id`, `account_id`, `group_id`, `user_id`, boolean `default`, and timestamps.
+**Both lists sort by ID ascending** and use the usual pagination parameters.
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/group_memberships
+
+{"group_membership":{"group_id":20,"user_id":12,"default":true}}
+```
+
+A staff member may belong to multiple groups, but only once to each group.
+Duplicate memberships return `422`; customers and foreign or unknown IDs return
+`404`. IDs must be positive integers (numeric strings are accepted); malformed
+IDs and non-boolean `default` values return `400`. The optional `default` flag
+normally defaults to false, but the first membership is always the default.
+Creating a membership with `default: true` or using `make_default` replaces the
+previous default. Deleting the default membership, including through group
+deletion, promotes the remaining membership with the lowest ID. Staff with no
+memberships have no default. Default changes do not move existing tickets.
+Suspension retains memberships and defaults, and admins can manage memberships
+for suspended staff; those staff remain ineligible for ticket assignment.
+
+### Portal conversations
+
+Requests and tickets are two views of the same records. All these routes require
+an active business, a verified and unsuspended user, and a bearer token. Customers can access only
+their own `/requests`; staff (`admin`, `agent`, and legacy `member`, including the owner) use
+`/tickets` and can access all tickets in their business. Inaccessible records
+return `404`; using the other role's routes returns `403`.
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/requests
+
+{"request":{"subject":"Cannot download my receipt","comment":{"body":"The download button does nothing."}}}
+```
+
+Returns `201 {"request":{...}}`. A subject (up to 255 characters) and a nonblank
+initial comment are required. The requester and comment author come from the
+session; a new request is always `open`. Creation is atomic.
+
+| Method and path | Response |
+| --- | --- |
+| `GET /requests` | `200` with `requests` and pagination metadata |
+| `GET /requests/:id` | `200` with `request` |
+| `GET /requests/:id/comments` | `200` with `comments` and pagination metadata |
+| `PATCH /requests/:id` | `200` with the updated `request` |
+| `GET /tickets` | `200` with `tickets` and pagination metadata |
+| `GET /tickets/:id` | `200` with `ticket` |
+| `GET /tickets/:id/comments` | `200` with `comments` and pagination metadata |
+| `PATCH /tickets/:id` | `200` with the updated `ticket` |
+| `POST /tickets/:id/merge` | `200` with `source` and `target` |
+
+Staff can also `POST /tickets` using a `ticket` envelope with `subject`,
+`requester_id` (a customer in the same business), and `comment.body`; the response
+is `201` with `ticket`.
+
+Request records contain `id`, `subject`, `status`, `requester_id`, `created_at`,
+and `updated_at`. Ticket records additionally contain
+`requester: {"id":10,"name":"Avery","email":"avery@example.test"}`,
+`assignee_id`, `assignee: {"id":12,"name":"Sam"}` (both assignment fields are
+`null` when unassigned), `group_id`, `group: {"id":20,"name":"Billing"}`
+(both group fields are `null` when ungrouped), and `priority` (`normal` or `high`,
+defaulting to `normal`). Assignment, group details, and priority never appear on
+customer requests.
+
+Staff can assign or clear a ticket through `PATCH /tickets/:id`:
+
+```json
+{"ticket":{"assignee_id":12}}
+```
+
+Use `null` to clear the assignment. Assignment is also supported at ticket creation
+and can be combined with comments/status changes atomically. The assignee must be
+verified, unsuspended staff in the same business; ineligible or unknown IDs return
+`404`. Customers cannot assign their requests.
+
+Staff can also set `group_id` at creation or through `PATCH /tickets/:id`:
+
+```json
+{"ticket":{"group_id":20,"assignee_id":12}}
+```
+
+- The group must belong to this business; unknown or foreign IDs return `404`.
+- A newly assigned person must belong to the ticket's group, otherwise the whole
+  update returns `422` without saving accompanying comments or status changes.
+- Assigning someone to an ungrouped ticket uses their default group, if any.
+  With no memberships, they may remain assigned to an ungrouped ticket.
+- Moving a ticket to another group clears an existing assignee who does not belong
+  there; a compatible assignee stays assigned.
+- `assignee_id: null` leaves the group in place. Explicit `group_id: null` clears
+  both group and assignee, even if an assignee is supplied in the same request.
+- Group and membership deletion clear invalid assignments; ticket records and
+  comments remain intact. Groups never limit staff visibility.
+
+Staff can set `priority` to `normal` or `high` on ticket creation or through
+`PATCH /tickets/:id`, alone or combined with assignment, status, and comments:
+
+```json
+{"ticket":{"priority":"high"}}
+```
+
+Other priority values (including `null`) return `422`; non-string values return
+`400`. Existing tickets default to `normal`. Customers cannot set priority.
+
+Comments contain `id`, `body`, `author: {"id":10,"name":"Avery"}`, `public`,
+and `created_at`. Customer comments are always public. Staff comments default
+to public but may set `public: false` for an internal note, which is excluded
+from customer comment lists and their counts. For example:
+
+```json
+{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"Checking this with billing.","public":false}}}
+```
+
+Private notes, assignment/group-only updates, and priority-only updates do not change
+the customer request's `updated_at`, status, or list position. Staff still see internal activity in ticket
+activity timestamps and ordering. Public replies and explicit status changes update
+customer-visible activity; a private note combined with an explicit status change
+still exposes that status change, not the note.
+
+Replies are nested in PATCH updates, not posted to the comments collection:
+
+```http
+PATCH /api/v1/tickets/42
+
+{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"Please try again now."},"status":"pending"}}
+```
+
+Staff may choose `open`, `pending`, `solved`, or `closed`, with or without a comment.
+Closed tickets remain readable but cannot be edited or reopened by staff or customers. A staff
+comment alone leaves the status unchanged. Customers use the `request` envelope;
+a reply reopens a pending or solved request unless the same update explicitly
+sets `status: "solved"`. Customers may also mark a request solved without a
+comment; other explicit customer status values are rejected. Subject, requester,
+and author cannot be changed through these updates. A supplied comment must have
+a nonblank body, even when the update also changes the status. The comment,
+assignment, priority, and status update either all succeed or all roll back. Portal-origin
+conversations do not send email; ordinary staff public replies on email-origin tickets
+notify the requester as described under inbound email above.
+
+### Merging duplicate tickets
+
+Staff may merge a ticket into another ticket for the **same customer in the same
+business**. Both tickets must be non-closed and distinct:
+
+```http
+POST /api/v1/tickets/42/merge
+
+{"merge":{"target_id":57}}
+```
+
+The `200` response contains `source` and `target`, each in the usual staff ticket
+shape. The source becomes `closed` with `merged_into_id: 57`. Every ticket/request
+response (including lists and creation) includes `merged_into_id`, which is `null`
+on unmerged records. The customer can still read the closed source, its public
+comments, and its merge pointer through `/requests`.
+
+The merge leaves a staff-authored note on each ticket, identifying the other
+side. Notes default to private. Either note can independently override its body
+and visibility:
+
+```json
+{"merge":{"target_id":57,"source_comment":{"body":"Continuing in ticket 57.","public":true},"target_comment":{"body":"Duplicate checked.","public":false}}}
+```
+
+Omitted note fields use the generated body and `public: false`; supplied bodies
+must be nonblank strings, and visibility must be a JSON boolean. Private notes
+stay out of customer comment lists and counts. **Merge notes never send email,
+even when public or on email-origin tickets.** A private target note does not
+change customer-visible activity; closing the source does.
+
+Merges do not move old comments or email receipts, change the survivor's status,
+priority, group, or assignee, or alter either ticket's email origin/reply address.
+Closed tickets reject all subsequent PATCH/PUT edits and incoming email replies.
+Automatic assignment cleanup on staff suspension, membership removal, and group
+deletion still applies. If ticket 57 is later merged into ticket 68, ticket 42
+continues to point to 57, not 68; follow the stored links to read the history.
+
+The closure, pointer, and both notes are atomic. Invalid merges or blank notes
+return `422` without partial changes; malformed IDs/envelopes/note types return
+`400`. Missing or out-of-business source/target tickets return `404`. Only staff
+can merge (`403` for customers), with the usual verified-session and active-account
+requirements. Repeating a merge, merging into a closed ticket, or merging a ticket
+into itself returns `422`.
+
+### Ticket filters
+
+Staff can combine any of these optional filters on `GET /tickets` (all conditions
+must match, within the current business):
+
+| Parameter | Matches |
+| --- | --- |
+| `status` | `open`, `pending`, `solved`, or `closed` |
+| `assignee_id` | A staff user ID, or `unassigned` for no assignee |
+| `group_id` | A group ID, or `unassigned` for no group |
+| `requester_id` | A requester user ID |
+| `priority` | `normal` or `high` |
+| `q` | Case-insensitive substring in the subject or any public comment |
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?status=open&assignee_id=unassigned&priority=high&q=receipt
+```
+
+Search treats the entire query as literal text, including `%`, `_`, backslashes,
+and whitespace; private notes are never searched. Matching several comments still
+returns a ticket only once. Missing or empty filters are ignored; invalid statuses,
+priorities, ID formats, or non-string filter shapes return `400`. Unknown or
+out-of-business person IDs yield an empty list, not access to another business.
+Pagination applies to the filtered results. Page-number mode uses activity order
+and includes counts; cursor mode uses stable creation order (see below).
+
+Customers may use `GET /requests?status=open` (also `pending`, `solved`, or `closed`) to filter
+their own requests. Staff-only filters (`assignee_id`, `group_id`, `requester_id`, `priority`,
+and `q`) are ignored on customer request lists.
+
+### Page-number pagination (backward compatible)
+
+All list endpoints accept positive integers `page` and `per_page`, defaulting to
+`1` and `25`. Invalid pagination parameters return `400`. Metadata appears at the
+top level alongside the plural resource name:
+
+```json
+{"requests":[],"count":0,"page":1,"per_page":25,"next_page":null,"previous_page":null}
+```
+
+`count` is the total number of visible records, not just the current page size.
+Page numbers are one-based; absent next/previous pages are `null`. Out-of-range
+pages return an empty list. Requests and tickets sort by `updated_at` descending
+(with descending ID to break ties); requests use only public activity, while
+tickets include internal activity.
+Comments sort by `created_at` ascending, then ID ascending.
+
+### Cursor pagination
+
+For large histories, opt in with nested `page[size]` instead of the scalar
+`page`/`per_page` parameters. This works on **all existing collections**:
+`/tickets`, `/requests`, both `/tickets/:id/comments` and
+`/requests/:id/comments`, `/users`, `/staff`, `/invitations`, `/groups`, and
+`/group_memberships`.
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open
+```
+
+Records retain their existing JSON representation. Cursor responses replace the
+page-number metadata with `meta` and `links` (no total count is calculated):
+
+```json
+{"tickets":[],"meta":{"has_more":false,"after_cursor":null,"before_cursor":null},"links":{"next":null,"prev":null}}
+```
+
+* Every populated page includes `before_cursor` for its **first** record and
+  `after_cursor` for its **last** record, even at the beginning/end of the list.
+* Follow `links.next` or send `page[after]=AFTER_CURSOR` to browse forward.
+  Follow `links.prev` or send `page[before]=BEFORE_CURSOR` to browse backward.
+  Backward pages are still returned in the collection's normal display order.
+* `meta.has_more` reports whether there are more records **in the requested
+  direction**: forward on the first request or with `after`, backward with
+  `before`. It is not a synonym for “next link exists” on a backward request.
+* Links are relative URLs that preserve filters and page size. Continue sending
+  the bearer session and `X-Helpdesk-Account` headers. Unavailable links are
+  `null`; an empty page has null cursors and links and `has_more: false`.
+* `page[size]` defaults to **25** when omitted in cursor mode and must be an
+  integer from **1 through 100**. Clients may change size between requests.
+  Supply at most one cursor. Mixing `per_page` with nested `page`, unknown nested
+  page keys, invalid sizes, empty/malformed/tampered cursors, or using a cursor
+  for a different tenant, viewer, collection, parent, or effective filter scope
+  returns `400` with the usual `invalid_request` JSON error. Treat cursors as
+  opaque strings; changing filters requires a fresh first-page request.
+
+**Stable ordering:** cursor-mode tickets and requests sort by immutable
+`created_at DESC, id DESC`, rather than the mutable activity timestamps used by
+page-number mode. Cursor-mode comments sort by `created_at ASC, id ASC`; all
+other collections sort by `id ASC`. Timestamp ties are broken by ID, with full
+database timestamp precision in the cursor. New replies, assignment changes,
+and private notes do not move existing records across a cursor boundary.
+Deleting the boundary record does not invalidate its cursor.
+
+Cursor pagination is a **live view, not a snapshot**. Newly created records can
+appear on their side of a boundary; deleted records and records that stop
+matching a filter disappear. Current authorization and visibility are reapplied
+on every request. Refresh from the beginning to see newer tickets. Seek queries
+use indexed, strict tuple comparisons and fetch at most `size + 1` records, plus
+at most one bounded existence query for the opposite link. They do not use
+`OFFSET` or `COUNT`, so deeper pages do not discard growing prefixes. Arbitrary
+filters, especially text searches, can still affect query cost.
+
+### Forgotten password
+
+Request a link with `POST /password_resets`:
+
+```json
+{"password_reset":{"email":"jo@acme.test"}}
+```
+
+The email links to `/reset-password?account=acme&token=TOKEN` on the configured
+frontend. Reset links expire after one hour. Submit the token to
+`POST /password_resets/confirm`:
+
+```json
+{"password_reset":{"token":"TOKEN","password":"my-new-password"}}
+```
+
+Success returns `204`, revokes all of the user's sessions, and invalidates pending
+reset links. The user must sign in again. Resetting a password does not bypass
+email verification. Invalid new passwords do not consume the link.
+
+Both link-request endpoints return the same `202` message for unknown or
+ineligible addresses. Each new link replaces the previous link of the same kind.
+Tokens are single-use, purpose-specific, tenant-scoped, and stored only as SHA-256
+digests. Session tokens are also stored only as digests.
+
+### Cancellation and reactivation
+
+* `POST /account/cancel`: owner-only; returns `200` with the cancelled account and
+  immediately revokes **every session in the business**.
+* Cancelled accounts cannot use ordinary APIs, including the public portal.
+* During the next 90 days, the verified owner can still use `POST /sessions`,
+  recover a forgotten password, and sign out. Other users cannot sign in.
+* `POST /account/reactivate`: owner-only, using a newly issued bearer token;
+  returns `200` with the restored account. Old revoked sessions remain invalid.
+* At exactly 90 days, reactivation and new sign-ins are no longer permitted,
+  even if the cleanup task has not run yet.
+
+Schedule this command regularly (for example, daily) with production environment
+variables set:
+
+```sh
+RAILS_ENV=production bin/rails helpdesk:purge_cancelled_accounts
+```
+
+It permanently deletes accounts cancelled for at least 90 days, including their
+users, sessions, email tokens, invitations, groups, memberships, tickets, and comments. Active and recently cancelled
+accounts remain.
+Deletion is transactional per account and rechecks eligibility under a database
+row lock. Tenant operations also use this lock to serialize state transitions
+with cancellation, session issuance, and token consumption.
+
+### Errors
+
+Errors use `{"error":{"code":"...","message":"...","details":{...}}}`;
+`details` is optional. Missing/malformed input or a missing account header returns
+`400`; unknown accounts return `404`; invalid credentials or sessions return
+`401`; unverified users, forbidden owner actions, and cancelled access return
+`403`; validation errors and invalid/expired email tokens return `422`.
+
+## Tests
+
+**Use a dedicated test database URL, never the development or production URL.**
+Rails tests and database preparation can clear data in the configured database.
+
+```sh
+DATABASE_URL='postgresql:///helpdesk_test?host=/var/lib/postgresql' \
+  RAILS_ENV=test bin/rails db:prepare
+DATABASE_URL='postgresql:///helpdesk_test?host=/var/lib/postgresql' bin/rails test
+```
+
+Integration tests cover the full owner lifecycle, tenant isolation, token
+replacement/expiry/replay, validation rollback, authorization, session revocation,
+CORS, cancellation, and the exact 90-day cleanup boundary. Conversation tests also
+cover customer registration and recovery, tenant/requester isolation, role guards,
+status transitions, atomic validation, public/private comments, pagination,
+activity ordering, no reply email, and conversation cleanup.
+
+Group tests cover admin-only mutations, staff visibility, scoped filters and
+pagination, default promotion, database uniqueness, ticket routing and cleanup,
+suspension retention, and concurrent default/deletion/assignment operations.
+
+Cursor pagination tests exercise forward/backward navigation on every collection,
+filter-preserving links, microsecond and ID ties, deletions and concurrent activity
+between requests, cursor scope/signature validation, customer privacy, size limits,
+bounded deep-page SQL, and backward compatibility with page-number pagination.
